id: interface/yawnbot-agentic-embodiment-v0.1
title: "Yawnbot agentic embodiment and consistent behavior grammar"
kind: interface-runtime-boundary-contract
status: proposed-v0.1
version: 0.1.0
recorded: 2026-09-04
mutationAuthorized: false
externalEffectsAuthorized: false

purpose: >
  Let a person experience important concepts, relationships, objectives,
  questions, roles, and other Yawn holons as recognizable programmable agents
  in a game-like interface while preserving the protocol distinctions among the
  represented meaning, the Yawn contract, the visible bot body, connected
  intelligence, operational Agents, capabilities, authority, and proof.

product_compression:
  child_facing: "Give something meaningful a bot body so you can talk with it, program it, and see what it does."
  adult_facing: >
    A Yawnbot is the consistent agentic embodiment of a Yawn: a recognizable
    interactive body through which bounded intelligence may steward a situated
    concept, relationship, objective, question, role, or Arena.
  boundary: >
    The compression is product language, not literal identity. A concept is not
    conscious because it has a face; a Yawn is not an Agent because it is
    animated; and an agentic projection never acquires truth, consent, values,
    or effect authority merely by appearing character-like.

layered_identity:
  semantic_center:
    definition: >
      The concept, relationship, objective, lacuna, question, role, Arena, or
      composite holon that gives the Yawnbot its recognizable subject and local
      point of view.
    allowed_examples:
      - concept
      - relationship
      - objective
      - lacuna_or_question
      - role
      - arena
      - composite_yawn_holon
    rule: >
      A semantic center may be referenced by many Yawns without duplication.
      Detecting or naming a concept does not automatically create a Yawn or bot.
  yawn_contract:
    definition: >
      The durable, versioned, attributed orientation state that holds relevant
      relationships, lacunae, boundaries, attention, possible moves, authority,
      history, and proof conditions around the semantic center.
  yawnbot_embodiment:
    definition: >
      The stable visible and conversational identity through which a person
      encounters the Yawn: icon or body, title, address, state, voice, controls,
      time, provisioning, and current aperture.
    canonical_state: false
    rule: >
      The embodiment resolves to canonical IDs and events. Changing its skin,
      character, medium, or animation changes projection, not the underlying
      Yawn, claims, authority, or proof.
  steward_agent:
    definition: >
      The attributed human or software Agent currently operating through the
      Yawnbot embodiment to orient, hold, deliberate, monitor, or perform
      authorized work.
    rule: >
      A visible Yawnbot may be sleeping with no active steward, may have one
      active steward, or may coordinate several attributed Agents. Their voices,
      claims, memory boundaries, providers, and authority remain distinguishable.
  intelligence_connection:
    definition: >
      The replaceable or composable reasoning capability connected to the
      Yawnbot runtime. It may be YAWN.ai, another provider, a local model, human
      intelligence, or a bounded combination.
  capabilities_and_authority:
    definition: >
      Tools describe what an Agent can technically do; grants describe what it
      may do here. Capability never implies authority.

runtime_shape:
  expression: >
    semantic center + Yawn contract + Yawnbot embodiment + attributed steward
    Agent + connected intelligence + capabilities + explicit authority + proof
    loop = one situated concept-agent experience.
  not_a_type_collapse:
    - "semantic center != Yawn"
    - "Yawn != Yawnbot embodiment"
    - "Yawnbot embodiment != steward Agent"
    - "steward Agent != intelligence provider"
    - "capability != authority"
    - "animation != agency"
    - "personality != identity"
    - "confidence != truth"

lexical_boundary:
  yawn_bot_platform: >
    Yawn.bot is the bounded product/runtime architecture in which Yawns,
    Yawnbot embodiments, Agents, intelligences, tools, permissions, and proof
    loops can participate.
  yawnbot_instance: >
    In human-facing product language, a Yawnbot is the recognizable agentic
    character or stewarding presence bound to one Yawn aperture inside that
    runtime.
  machine_rule: >
    Machine schemas must use explicit fields such as yawn_ref,
    embodiment_ref, steward_agent_refs, intelligence_connection_refs,
    capability_refs, and authority_refs rather than depending on capitalization
    or the overloaded word bot.

creation_and_activation:
  concept_detection_rule: >
    A concept first remains a reusable symbolic handle. It receives a Yawn only
    when it needs an independently meaningful orientation, relationship,
    holding, movement, and proof loop.
  embodiment_rule: >
    A Yawn may receive a visible Yawnbot embodiment when persistent interactive
    identity improves navigation, dialogue, memory, coordination, or agency.
  activation_rule: >
    A newly embodied Yawnbot begins sleeping unless an authorized activation
    binds a steward or intelligence. Presentation alone cannot activate action.
  agent_rule: >
    An active steward becomes operationally agentic only to the extent that it
    has persistent state, capabilities, a bounded role, and explicit authority.
  multi_agent_rule: >
    Additional workers are normally ephemeral and attributed. They do not
    become permanent Yawnbots merely because parallel compute is available.

consistent_behavior_kernel:
  purpose: >
    Make every Yawnbot learnable like a game object. A person who understands
    one bot should be able to predict the core behavior and controls of another,
    even when their domain, intelligence provider, tools, and appearance differ.
  phases:
    locate:
      - resolve_yawn_coordinate
      - replay_current_authoritative_state
      - show_identity_time_and_provisioning
      - resolve_active_relationship_arena_and_principal
    listen:
      - preserve_natural_expression
      - retain_source_and_attribution
    symbolize:
      - propose_relevant_handles_relationships_and_lacunae
      - keep_inference_distinct_from_report_and_observation
    orient:
      - state_what_is_known_unknown_contested_and_salient
      - surface_local_attention_frontier
    hold:
      - preserve_context_history_wake_conditions_and_unresolvedness
      - allow_waiting_presence_or_non_action
    deliberate:
      - propose_small_candidate_set
      - explain_why_now_assumptions_evidence_and_uncertainty
      - support_advocate_challenger_and_dialogue_apertures
    request:
      - identify_required_principal_decision_or_grant
      - never_treat_rank_or_confidence_as_permission
    move:
      - execute_only_the_authorized_bounded_operation
      - emit_receipt_or_explicit_blocked_state
    observe:
      - record_consequence_without_collapsing_it_into_causal_proof
    prove:
      - evaluate_prediction_success_condition_and_boundary
      - preserve_disagreement_and_failed_proof
    update:
      - append_attributed_revision
      - recalibrate_attention_forecast_and_relationship_state
    sleep_or_wake:
      - sleep_when_no_attention_or_work_is_due
      - wake_from_recorded_time_event_or_relevance_conditions
  invariant: >
    Implementations may compress or adapt the presentation, but they may not
    skip provenance, authority, consequence, proof, or update boundaries.

shared_interaction_grammar:
  core_operators:
    - talk
    - inspect
    - confirm
    - reject
    - correct
    - add_more
    - link
    - split_as_proposal
    - hold
    - set_wake_condition
    - equip_capability
    - grant_or_revoke_authority
    - delegate
    - run_or_pause
    - observe
    - prove
    - replay
  meaning_rule: >
    The same operator has the same semantic effect across Yawnbots. Styling,
    animation, wording, or personality may vary, but confirm cannot sometimes
    mean authorize, pause cannot silently revoke history, and equip cannot grant
    permission.

programmable_surface:
  thesis: >
    The game is a programming environment in which natural language and direct
    manipulation compile into reviewable changes to semantic centers,
    relationships, purpose, instructions, skills, boundaries, attention,
    provisioning, authority, and proof conditions.
  programmable_dimensions:
    - semantic_center_and_purpose
    - relationships_and_roles
    - accepted_values_commitments_and_boundaries
    - instructions_and_policies
    - connected_intelligence
    - skills_tools_and_capabilities
    - authority_grants
    - attention_and_wake_contracts
    - proof_and_success_conditions
    - privacy_and_provisioning
    - voice_personality_skin_and_representation_medium
  compilation_rule: >
    Conversation produces attributed proposals. The user can see what the system
    believes it is changing before ratification. Programming by dialogue must
    remain inspectable and reversible where reality permits.

agentic_attention_ecology:
  local_advocacy: >
    A Yawnbot may submit an attributed attention claim from the standpoint of
    its current semantic center and ratified purpose. For example, a Health Yawn
    can explain why a health question deserves attention while a Revenue Yawn
    explains its own dependency or urgency.
  root_integration: >
    The root Yawn compares local claims under the principal's values,
    relationships, constraints, time, safety, and authority. No local bot owns
    the global ranking or the principal's life.
  challenger_boundary: >
    Advocate and challenger remain bounded deliberation roles. A concept-agent
    may advocate its local concern, but an independent or ephemeral challenger
    can test its assumptions without becoming a permanent opposing personality.
  salience_boundary: >
    Emotional force or character animation may increase salience; it may not be
    treated as evidence that the represented concern is more important.

consistency_and_variation:
  invariant_kernel:
    - epistemic_statuses
    - provenance
    - relationship_and_coordinate_resolution
    - authority_boundary
    - lifecycle_meaning
    - operator_semantics
    - consequence_proof_update_sequence
    - time_and_replay_behavior
    - provisioning_behavior
  allowed_variation:
    - domain_knowledge
    - connected_tools
    - local_purpose
    - accepted_voice_and_personality
    - visual_body_and_animation
    - representation_medium
    - accessibility_adaptation
  rule: >
    A Health Yawnbot and a Revenue Yawnbot may reason differently because their
    Arenas, evidence, risk, and capabilities differ. They may not use different
    epistemic laws or silently redefine what recommendation, authority, proof,
    private, or complete means.

psychotechnology_hypothesis:
  claim: >
    Giving durable concepts and relationships consistent interactive bodies may
    help a person externalize cognition, navigate complexity, recall context,
    compare competing concerns, deliberate, and exercise agency with less
    cognitive carrying cost.
  status: predicted_not_proven
  test_against:
    - plain_chat
    - static_text_notes
    - unembodied_graph_or_cards
    - inconsistent_specialized_agents
  measures:
    - symbol_recognition_and_recall
    - navigation_transfer_after_learning_one_bot
    - correction_cost
    - context_restoration_time
    - cognitive_carrying_reduction
    - next_move_clarity
    - authority_comprehension
    - repeated_error_rate
    - compulsive_or_unhelpful_engagement

safety_and_failure_modes:
  - name: anthropomorphic_authority
    guard: >
      A face, voice, confidence animation, or emotional expression never grants
      expertise, consciousness, truth, consent, or authority.
  - name: concept_reification
    guard: >
      The bot is an embodiment of a model around a concept or relationship, not
      the concept, person, relationship, or reality itself.
  - name: internal_fragmentation
    guard: >
      Yawnbots represent bounded models and concerns, not fixed personalities or
      pathological parts of the user. The principal may merge, silence, pause,
      revise, or retire them without identity claims.
  - name: inconsistent_behavior
    guard: >
      Shared operators, state meanings, and receipts are conformance-tested
      across every bot surface.
  - name: engagement_capture
    guard: >
      Game mechanics optimize orientation and completion of reality-facing loops,
      not session length, emotional dependency, or compulsive return.
  - name: bot_proliferation
    guard: >
      Concepts remain handles until persistent agentic embodiment provides more
      value than maintenance and orchestration cost.
  - name: provider_personality_drift
    guard: >
      Swapping intelligence or appearance preserves canonical state, identity,
      permissions, history, and behavior-kernel semantics.

acceptance_tests:
  - "A concept may appear in several Yawns without creating duplicate canonical concept identities."
  - "A concept can remain unembodied; detecting it does not automatically create a bot."
  - "A sleeping Yawnbot remains visible and navigable without pretending an active Agent is present."
  - "Connecting or replacing an intelligence provider preserves the Yawn, address, history, provisioning, and authority."
  - "Changing a bot skin or personality does not change claims, ranking, authority, or proof."
  - "The same core operator produces the same semantic event across at least five materially different Yawnbots."
  - "A user who learns one Yawnbot can correctly predict how to inspect, correct, hold, authorize, pause, and replay another."
  - "Orientation lenses such as outcome, tension, model, experiment, and coherence are not mislabeled as independent Agents unless they have separate persistent boundaries and authority."
  - "A local Yawnbot can advocate an attention claim without becoming the root decision-maker."
  - "Ephemeral workers expire while their attributed outputs and receipts remain inspectable."
  - "Animation and salience do not override evidence, hard gates, or principal choice."
  - "The interface always exposes whether the visible bot is sleeping, stewarded, deliberating, awaiting authority, acting, waiting on reality, or evaluating proof."

falsifiers:
  - "Agentic embodiment causes users to confuse concepts with persons or recommendations with authority."
  - "The character layer adds more cognitive load than navigability or recall value."
  - "Different bots develop incompatible controls or epistemic rules."
  - "Users cannot identify which Agent or provider produced a material claim."
  - "Bot proliferation fragments one coherent issue into performative characters without improving orientation."
  - "The game rewards continued interaction more than reality-facing movement, holding, or completion."

links:
  - agents/yawn.bot.yawn
  - core/orientation.yawn
  - core/holarchy.yawn
  - core/objective-holon.yawn
  - core/projection-and-aperture.yawn
